WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.398
Arithmetic behavior is a design decision.

2
00:00:02.478 --> 00:00:06.115
Overflow and underflow happen when a
calculation crosses the range that a type can

3
00:00:06.195 --> 00:00:06.722
represent.

4
00:00:06.802 --> 00:00:09.964
The defensive question is not only whether
Rust catches a problem in one

5
00:00:10.044 --> 00:00:10.782
configuration.

6
00:00:10.862 --> 00:00:14.341
The question is which arithmetic behavior
the application actually intends.

7
00:00:14.421 --> 00:00:16.793
Rust can detect overflow in checked
configurations,

8
00:00:16.873 --> 00:00:20.616
but production code should not rely on
accidental build-mode behavior for security

9
00:00:20.696 --> 00:00:24.280
decisions, allocation decisions,
authorization rules, or parser boundaries.

10
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.050
If failure matters, the code should
represent failure explicitly through checked

11
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.397
arithmetic or a domain-specific validation
path.

12
00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:33.850
Checked arithmetic reports failure when
the result cannot be represented.

13
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.146
That is often the right choice for
allocation sizes, offsets, counters,

14
00:00:37.226 --> 00:00:39.229
protocol calculations, money-like values,

15
00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:42.788
and other cases where crossing the
boundary means the operation should stop or

16
00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:43.606
return an error.

17
00:00:43.686 --> 00:00:45.689
Saturating arithmetic clamps at a
boundary.

18
00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:49.195
It can be appropriate for some counters,
metrics, or bounded display values,

19
00:00:49.275 --> 00:00:52.490
but it can hide a problem if the domain
really needs to know that a limit was

20
00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.045
exceeded.

21
00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:56.762
Wrapping arithmetic intentionally wraps
around the type range and should be used

22
00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:00.057
only when wraparound is part of the
design, not because it is convenient.

23
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:03.458
Overflowing arithmetic returns both a
result and an overflow indication,

24
00:01:03.538 --> 00:01:06.595
which can be useful when the caller needs
both pieces of information.

25
00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:09.996
Whichever behavior is chosen, document the
intent and test the edge cases.

26
00:01:10.076 --> 00:01:11.499
Minimums, maximums, zero, one,

27
00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:14.900
and one-past-limit values are where
numeric assumptions become visible.
